WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:14.880
We're going to talk about graph engineering today.

00:14.880 --> 00:17.720
How many people have heard about that term, graph engineering?

00:17.720 --> 00:20.000
Okay, so a fair amount of room.

00:20.000 --> 00:24.240
And then just so I understand, how many people here are familiar with Neo4j and have used

00:24.240 --> 00:25.240
Neo4j?

00:25.240 --> 00:27.560
Okay, so we've got a few people.

00:27.560 --> 00:36.760
So let's go for just letting me know who we're talking to today.

00:36.760 --> 00:41.800
So just some history on some of the verb digits being used.

00:41.800 --> 00:48.440
There was a tweet that went out by Peter Steinberger, I think, earlier this year in the summer,

00:48.440 --> 00:52.680
that talked about graph engineering, and it was almost like, is this sort of a new discipline

00:52.680 --> 00:57.880
that's happening as this evolution from prompt engineering and context engineering.

00:57.880 --> 01:01.040
And there's a lot of hype around this.

01:01.040 --> 01:04.480
And the name for this is new, but the mechanisms are not really new.

01:04.480 --> 01:10.240
We've always known in computer science generally about state machines and workflow graphs and

01:10.240 --> 01:12.960
knowledge graphs and all these different things.

01:12.960 --> 01:18.880
What's changing is really how we think about them in reference to agents.

01:18.880 --> 01:23.200
And what I'm going to talk to you about today is just how we think about graph engineering.

01:23.200 --> 01:27.000
And I'm going to offer you a little bit of a new perspective on this.

01:27.000 --> 01:31.760
So if we actually try to define what graph engineering is, I think a good definition

01:31.760 --> 01:37.000
is it's just the practice of designing agent work as an explicit graph.

01:37.000 --> 01:42.760
So where nodes do work and edges effectively route state between them.

01:42.760 --> 01:47.280
And I tend to think of it in terms of three different types of graphs.

01:47.280 --> 01:52.560
We have our general execution graphs, which you're probably very familiar with.

01:52.560 --> 01:58.400
So these are the workflow graphs with routers and agents as actual nodes that happen with

01:58.400 --> 02:00.800
inside of an agent harness.

02:00.800 --> 02:05.760
But there's other ways that we can think about graph engineering too that overlap with this.

02:05.760 --> 02:08.200
So that includes context graphs.

02:08.200 --> 02:13.480
These are decision traces, instances of what's happened, and how it connects to other data

02:13.480 --> 02:15.320
inside of your domain.

02:15.320 --> 02:19.280
And then there's also something that I'd call more procedural graphs.

02:19.280 --> 02:25.360
So this is almost like having a skill or procedural knowledge as a graph, where the steps might

02:25.360 --> 02:32.200
not be actual agents, but they might be steps inside of a way of doing something basically

02:32.200 --> 02:33.200
a procedure.

02:33.200 --> 02:38.760
So different calls, maybe a step for a decision model, steps for different agents, whether

02:38.760 --> 02:42.840
to escalate validation steps and these sorts of things.

02:42.840 --> 02:47.120
And I'm really going to focus on these last two because I think we don't talk about them

02:47.120 --> 02:48.120
enough.

02:48.120 --> 02:52.280
And I think by understanding these, you can actually get a little bit at what the first

02:52.280 --> 02:57.800
graph is trying to do as well, but in a very portable and efficient way.

02:57.800 --> 03:02.920
So graph engineering lets agents really take on bigger, more complex work.

03:02.920 --> 03:04.880
And that's why we care about it.

03:04.880 --> 03:08.880
And really, if you were to split it up, I think in terms of the benefits that a graph

03:08.880 --> 03:13.520
offers agents in this context, it would be predictability.

03:13.520 --> 03:18.760
So being able to constrain a path that an agent takes, making sure that the answer

03:18.760 --> 03:24.720
is basically accurate from a task that it's doing and having an explicit graph, the ability

03:24.720 --> 03:29.960
to govern, control, and guide what the agent does and audit it so you can understand what

03:29.960 --> 03:32.280
it's doing and make it better next time.

03:32.280 --> 03:35.920
And then the third one, which is going to be interesting that we'll talk about is how

03:35.920 --> 03:40.520
do we actually make graph engineering portable, and instead of thinking about a graph as something

03:40.520 --> 03:46.640
that happens in the runtime, it could be something that you can store and actually move between

03:46.640 --> 03:51.140
different runtimes and have inside of a knowledge layer, which gives you more interoperability

03:51.140 --> 03:54.760
and allows you to have a very simple stack.

03:54.760 --> 03:58.840
And in the runtime, when we think about the graph, it's really like a piece of orchestration.

03:58.840 --> 04:03.800
So probably all of us, or a large portion of us are probably familiar with LangGraph,

04:03.800 --> 04:11.200
ADK, and all these other agent frameworks, even to a certain extent, Anthropics, multi-agent

04:11.200 --> 04:15.800
research, and some of their stuff fits inside of graph engineering.

04:15.800 --> 04:19.400
And what I'm going to talk about today is actually what happens when we take that sort

04:19.400 --> 04:24.240
of graph and we put it next to our data as context graphs and some of the things that

04:24.240 --> 04:27.600
I was talking about before.

04:27.600 --> 04:32.680
And really when you do this, as I was saying, you add this portability layer.

04:32.680 --> 04:38.760
You add the ability to take all of this knowledge and make it work, basically, with any type

04:38.760 --> 04:39.760
of runtime.

04:39.760 --> 04:42.120
So that's what I'm going to go over today.

04:42.120 --> 04:47.160
And this is what we're doing at Neo4j is we're defining this knowledge layer.

04:47.160 --> 04:51.320
And this knowledge layer sits between systems of record that you would ordinarily retrieve

04:51.320 --> 04:52.560
data from.

04:52.560 --> 04:55.880
So Databix, BigQuery, Snowflake, these types of places.

04:55.880 --> 04:56.880
It could be documentation.

04:56.880 --> 05:00.560
It could be obsidian, these markdown files.

05:00.560 --> 05:05.760
And then can we create standard protocols and artifacts that live inside of that layer

05:05.760 --> 05:11.840
that then you can take a graph engineering process and make it available to any runtime

05:11.840 --> 05:15.320
or any agent?

05:15.320 --> 05:21.120
And the way our product works, just so you know at Neo4j, is we have our core database

05:21.120 --> 05:24.920
and then we have a ton of other AI stuff on top of it.

05:24.920 --> 05:29.560
So the database and the analytics layer, I'm going to be showing some of this throughout

05:29.560 --> 05:31.040
my talk.

05:31.040 --> 05:34.920
But what I'm really going to be focusing on is this top layer where we talk about sort

05:34.920 --> 05:40.240
of this agentic brain or these other memory tools that wrap around the database.

05:40.240 --> 05:44.480
So just know that as I go through, I'm going to be showing you data being loaded into the

05:44.480 --> 05:45.480
database.

05:45.480 --> 05:47.000
That's going to constitute our knowledge layer.

05:47.000 --> 05:50.480
It's going to come from multiple different sources.

05:50.480 --> 05:54.840
But a lot of the sort of extensions of that tooling and some of the experimental things

05:54.840 --> 05:59.200
that I'm going to show you exist as sort of this graph AI that hooks into that and loads

05:59.200 --> 06:02.900
data in and out of that thing.

06:02.900 --> 06:07.560
So what we're doing at Neo4j and kind of how we're thinking about this, we start with this

06:07.560 --> 06:13.760
concept of a context graph, which has different types of memory from short-term memory, which

06:13.760 --> 06:19.360
is going to be like your conversation history, your long-term memory being entities that

06:19.360 --> 06:24.360
are extracted from that or from your data, and then reasoning or decision traces inside

06:24.360 --> 06:25.840
of that.

06:25.840 --> 06:30.240
And one of the interesting things that we've been looking at from a research perspective

06:30.240 --> 06:35.520
and this really started out not so much at that graph, but actually at individual skill

06:35.520 --> 06:36.520
level.

06:36.520 --> 06:41.640
There was a paper that we wrote earlier this year that we published in a workshop, and we

06:41.640 --> 06:46.440
were really trying to ask the question, can we take procedural knowledge, whether it be

06:46.440 --> 06:51.960
from past decisions or even something like a skill like we have here, and compile it

06:51.960 --> 06:53.800
down to a graph.

06:53.800 --> 06:59.200
And what we came up with was a process of taking written material, often a skill MD file and

06:59.200 --> 07:04.640
the scripts and the references and assets, and moving that into an actual graph structure,

07:04.640 --> 07:07.520
which is what you see on that other side.

07:07.520 --> 07:12.240
And the result of doing this and giving the agent the access to that graph structure,

07:12.240 --> 07:18.200
which has different nodes, again representing inside of the skill decisions, different routing

07:18.240 --> 07:24.160
points, different scripts that have to be executed, taking pros and compiling them down

07:24.160 --> 07:26.680
to deterministic scripts.

07:26.680 --> 07:32.320
When we do that and we force the agent to sort of run that graph, what we see is increases

07:32.320 --> 07:34.920
in task reward and performance.

07:34.920 --> 07:41.480
So this was from a while ago, I am still working on updating it to the latest models, but we

07:41.480 --> 07:43.960
were able to use a smaller model.

07:43.960 --> 07:49.400
In this case, I think it was Sonnet, and now we are on to Haiku, as a matter of fact.

07:49.400 --> 07:55.200
But we were seeing, even for really challenging tasks over diverse fields in physics and computer

07:55.200 --> 08:04.520
science and various security, physical training, a whole ton of things, is this overall increase

08:04.520 --> 08:10.920
in task reward performance when we were able to switch over to this.

08:11.040 --> 08:16.400
And our PMs, Will Lyne, who works on our labs team, actually took this idea and said,

08:16.400 --> 08:22.160
well, if we can do that with skills, maybe we can do it too with these memory graphs.

08:22.160 --> 08:27.400
So you can actually take that context graph that I was showing you earlier and distill

08:27.400 --> 08:33.960
a skill from that and have it still be connected in the same graph over to all of your past

08:33.960 --> 08:35.960
decision traces and data.

08:35.960 --> 08:39.880
And so then you get those performance benefits, but you also have all this provenance that

08:39.920 --> 08:41.440
goes back from every step.

08:41.440 --> 08:45.840
It's versioned, it's tracked, and you have governance over time where you can assess

08:45.840 --> 08:50.480
drift, you can understand where certain tool calls are coming from in their history.

08:50.480 --> 08:55.360
A human can go through, actually, inside of NAMS, Neo4j agent memory service, which is

08:55.360 --> 08:57.040
our reference architecture for this.

08:57.040 --> 09:02.680
There's a QR code there, and there will be another one at the end, and be able to understand

09:02.680 --> 09:08.360
exactly how this works, edit it, change it, do all sorts of cool stuff with it.

09:08.400 --> 09:12.160
So again, what we're talking about now are these artifacts that you can move around.

09:12.160 --> 09:14.720
I mean, this thing makes a skill, right?

09:14.720 --> 09:18.840
And so any agent harness that can take that skill can now be running these graphs and

09:18.840 --> 09:22.600
doing all of this logic.

09:22.600 --> 09:29.360
And we have various customers that are using us for graph engineering types of tasks.

09:29.360 --> 09:34.120
I'll go quickly because we're starting to run out of time.

09:34.120 --> 09:40.600
And even though they may not be using exactly the LABS project that we have, we have consultancies

09:40.600 --> 09:45.600
which are working with other asset managers for tough problems in compliance.

09:45.600 --> 09:49.480
So being able to write audit reports, for example, where you have data that might come

09:49.480 --> 09:51.240
from multiple sources.

09:51.240 --> 09:54.800
How do you automate that, but do it in a way that's governed and trustworthy?

09:54.800 --> 09:59.400
So you have compliance documentation, you might have entities with different ticker symbols

09:59.400 --> 10:03.280
and names from different databases, and those all need to be resolved.

10:03.280 --> 10:08.760
And so giving your agent these graphs that express the logic that you need to do on besides

10:08.760 --> 10:13.920
the data basically allows you to automate those things in a trustworthy way.

10:13.920 --> 10:19.080
And we have other people, such as Uber, that do this with configuration graphs.

10:19.080 --> 10:24.920
So basically, you can imagine if you have drivers across the country, compliance and

10:24.920 --> 10:30.620
regulations for different areas geographically very greatly, and they come from this sprawl

10:30.620 --> 10:34.220
of microservices that you need to pull data from to figure out, like, okay, well, what

10:34.220 --> 10:39.140
is it for each area, for each component of a car, for example.

10:39.140 --> 10:44.100
And so being able to express that logic and have it sit aside the data also helps them

10:44.100 --> 10:47.420
operationalize some of these things effectively.

10:47.420 --> 10:50.540
So I'm going to jump to a demo now.

10:50.540 --> 10:53.940
I'm going to warn you in advance it's going to be quite a lot, and I'm going to jump through

10:53.940 --> 10:55.660
it kind of quickly.

10:55.660 --> 11:00.600
But it's very interesting, and what I'm going to show you is how we can use that AIP library

11:00.880 --> 11:06.120
that I was talking about before to compile a skill and actually get a task that was not

11:06.120 --> 11:12.360
governed very well and took very long to run, basically cut its cost by like 100% and then

11:12.360 --> 11:15.000
run it a lot, lot faster.

11:15.000 --> 11:19.160
And of course, it's a demo, it's not a formal research proposal, but you can go there and

11:19.160 --> 11:20.160
look at the code.

11:20.160 --> 11:22.760
It's all very transparent.

11:22.760 --> 11:29.500
So this is going to be an example for a loan, a loan approval process.

11:29.500 --> 11:34.660
So in the side of a loan approval process, there's lots of policies that have to be followed,

11:34.660 --> 11:35.660
obviously.

11:35.660 --> 11:39.180
I'm not a financial expert, but many of you in the room are.

11:39.180 --> 11:43.140
And so there's probably going to be a policy doc, there's going to be a systems of steps

11:43.140 --> 11:46.980
that have to be followed exactly, and then you need your agent to be able to explain

11:46.980 --> 11:51.460
those steps very clearly, articulate them, and really get the answer right as if it was

11:51.460 --> 11:53.580
a human reviewing.

11:53.580 --> 11:57.480
And the first thing you can do, like I was saying before, and I don't have time to probably

11:57.480 --> 12:00.560
run this all live and the internet's going to be slow.

12:00.560 --> 12:03.820
So luckily, I have this preloaded.

12:03.820 --> 12:08.360
And basically, the question that was asked was, should we approve this new loan for this

12:08.360 --> 12:10.120
roses bakery business?

12:10.120 --> 12:17.600
And in this case, we have a business plan that's in a PDF that was uploaded.

12:17.600 --> 12:22.920
And that is, let's see, the business plan is the orange data.

12:22.920 --> 12:26.560
So if I was to scroll out here, you can see it here in the graph.

12:26.600 --> 12:32.040
That connects also to data that we pulled from data breaks, from past transactions, lending

12:32.040 --> 12:36.320
policy data, and past decisions that were actually made.

12:36.320 --> 12:42.200
And with that, the agent can crawl over all that information and basically realize that

12:42.200 --> 12:47.880
this person either had a loan before or there was a mistake before, something happened before,

12:47.880 --> 12:54.440
and come up with an audit trail for that and basically post a solution very, very quickly.

12:54.440 --> 12:56.160
And this is a great start.

12:56.160 --> 13:00.120
In terms of like resolving data and being able to follow policies and understand how

13:00.120 --> 13:03.000
things connect, this is great.

13:03.000 --> 13:07.480
One of the issues with this is, and I can show you a graph kind of of what this looks

13:07.480 --> 13:08.480
like.

13:08.480 --> 13:12.040
This is an example of me running more of these policies.

13:12.040 --> 13:19.280
And if I zoom in, I have a conversation here, and then I have my reasoning trace that exists

13:19.280 --> 13:20.280
inside of this graph.

13:20.280 --> 13:24.200
And all of these steps are time stamped, and they have tool calls.

13:24.200 --> 13:33.960
What can happen is, if I look at this happening twice, I think this is the right query.

13:33.960 --> 13:36.600
This is two decision chains, so it's going to be a little bit hard to see.

13:36.600 --> 13:39.160
Let me zoom in.

13:39.160 --> 13:41.480
There are different lengths, you'll notice.

13:41.480 --> 13:44.480
So one is longer than the other.

13:44.480 --> 13:48.760
In this case, it was because it found a president in one, basically something else that had

13:48.760 --> 13:50.360
happened before.

13:50.760 --> 13:55.120
The problem with this is that, if you run these over and over again, there's nothing

13:55.120 --> 13:59.040
actually enforcing that the agent follows an exact path.

13:59.040 --> 14:02.920
You might have a skill or something that will say, like, do these things.

14:02.920 --> 14:06.400
But then there's other problems where it's like there's these decision steps where it's

14:06.400 --> 14:11.040
approve or deny is a simple one, or approve or send to a human to review.

14:11.040 --> 14:15.200
And those decision steps as well come with confidence scores.

14:15.200 --> 14:18.760
Or you say, like, if you're confident about it, if you're not confident about something,

14:18.760 --> 14:21.160
but those decisions aren't calibrated.

14:21.160 --> 14:24.480
So it would be great to, like, even take some of this incentive to something like a type

14:24.480 --> 14:30.280
safe, you know, JEV type of model as well, to be able to do some of those things.

14:30.280 --> 14:35.320
So what you can do is you can scrape all of this and you can compile it into a graph-shaped

14:35.320 --> 14:37.720
skill, which is what we did here.

14:37.720 --> 14:42.200
And that graph-shaped skill, if I go here, you're not going to be able to see it.

14:42.200 --> 14:45.640
But that's okay that you don't see a lot of the detail here.

14:45.640 --> 14:50.740
The important part is that we sort of compiled all the information down from those successful

14:50.740 --> 14:54.000
runs that we did, even though they were a little bit inconsistent.

14:54.000 --> 14:58.440
And we put them into this graph YAML shape inside of this skill.

14:58.440 --> 15:03.080
And this skill, even though you probably have trouble seeing, has scripts.

15:03.080 --> 15:05.640
It has assets here.

15:05.640 --> 15:08.000
It has sometimes other reference resources.

15:08.000 --> 15:09.840
It doesn't for this one.

15:09.840 --> 15:14.920
It also has source documentation around the policy that you're supposed to do for the

15:15.800 --> 15:19.800
So there's basically a ton of stuff in here for traceability, but then also telling the

15:19.800 --> 15:21.720
agent how to run this skill.

15:21.720 --> 15:26.760
But the great thing is, once the skill is in this YAML format, we can port it into all

15:26.760 --> 15:30.840
this different tooling, including inside of a graph, because it's all sort of this deterministic

15:30.840 --> 15:32.800
format.

15:32.800 --> 15:37.160
And in a graph, it looks kind of like this thing here.

15:37.160 --> 15:42.720
So let me, actually let me just run this sucker again.

15:42.720 --> 15:44.680
This is kind of what it looks like.

15:44.680 --> 15:50.480
So you have all of these steps out here, and then basically what I just did is I wrapped

15:50.480 --> 15:54.600
a small client around this so the agent can say, hey, run the skill.

15:54.600 --> 15:59.720
And instead of the agent deciding all the routing steps, it's just done programmatically.

15:59.720 --> 16:05.640
We took a lot of that policy down and we put a lot of it into these execution scripts.

16:05.640 --> 16:11.280
Basically what's happening here, if I was just to sum it up, there's an execution that

16:11.280 --> 16:17.360
basically looks at, hey, do we meet certain hard rules around policies?

16:17.360 --> 16:18.840
After that, it's routed.

16:18.840 --> 16:23.760
If it's not auto-approved or denied based on that, it's routed to a decision model,

16:23.760 --> 16:26.640
like JAV or TypeSafe.

16:26.640 --> 16:31.920
And I can even probably show you a little bit of a clear view here.

16:31.920 --> 16:36.040
And it's given that that model is given a risk to your choice.

16:37.040 --> 16:38.440
Let me make that a little bigger.

16:38.440 --> 16:38.960
Hold on.

16:42.880 --> 16:47.440
Whether or not there's some exposure material and then an outcome of whether or not you're

16:47.440 --> 16:49.240
approving or denying.

16:49.240 --> 16:54.320
And then the rest of this will basically follow down into writing a report.

16:54.320 --> 16:58.000
Whether or not you need to escalate to a human review is this router.

16:58.000 --> 17:00.760
And then if not, basically the agent's going to write a report.

17:00.760 --> 17:05.400
And there's a circulation here to sort of check and lint that report.

17:05.400 --> 17:10.400
So once you do this, and you sort of get the model going where, OK, now we have this

17:10.400 --> 17:13.160
client that we can just run this thing in.

17:13.160 --> 17:19.720
Well, now I can go in here and then maybe I can probably don't have time to run this

17:19.720 --> 17:24.760
whole thing again, but I will give it a shot.

17:24.760 --> 17:26.600
I'll see if it goes and picks this up.

17:26.600 --> 17:29.400
It takes about two minutes to run.

17:29.400 --> 17:34.640
But the idea, I guess, while this is running, and it should pick up the AIP skill, which

17:34.640 --> 17:40.960
it is here, once you have it in this format, not only can you have the agent run this very

17:40.960 --> 17:43.920
quickly, you can track the runs.

17:43.920 --> 17:51.680
So for example, if I was to look at a single run, I can run this again.

17:51.680 --> 17:55.520
I could basically persist the runs inside of the graph.

17:55.520 --> 18:00.920
So this is a run of that skill with individual steps that it ran.

18:00.920 --> 18:03.560
And then I can just persist these over time.

18:03.560 --> 18:05.200
I can examine how well they work.

18:05.200 --> 18:09.760
So from a governance perspective, I have a little inspector here that I built really

18:09.760 --> 18:10.760
quickly.

18:10.760 --> 18:13.080
You can look at the status of all these.

18:13.080 --> 18:17.600
You can look at how a specific one ran, what was approved, what was denied inside of the

18:17.600 --> 18:20.560
process, what branches it took.

18:20.560 --> 18:23.960
And then once you have a graph, you can even do these cool observability things where you

18:23.960 --> 18:29.560
can ask, well, like what branches were taken and which branches weren't taken, which is

18:29.560 --> 18:34.680
very interesting from a governance perspective, it failed ever, all these cool different things.

18:34.680 --> 18:38.760
And all of that is stored besides your data.

18:38.760 --> 18:43.560
And so in none of this, we're not creating a loop inside of our agent.

18:43.560 --> 18:48.840
All we're doing here is basically giving it a client to run this programmatic skill that

18:48.840 --> 18:54.000
we compiled from documentation, or in this case, from our memory graph.

18:54.000 --> 18:59.440
And this might take a while to run just because of the internet.

